  "contracts": {
    "lem-contrastive-hinge-and-logistic-loss-properties": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-derivative-algebra-and-chain-rules",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every function differentiable at $c$ is continuous at $c$.\nIf $f,g$ are real functions differentiable at $c$, then $f+g$ and $fg$ are\ndifferentiable there and\n$$\n(f+g)'(c)=f'(c)+g'(c),\\qquad (fg)'(c)=f'(c)g(c)+f(c)g'(c).\n$$\nIf $g(c)\\ne0$, then $f/g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n$$\n(f/g)'(c)=\\frac{f'(c)g(c)-f(c)g'(c)}{g(c)^2}.\n$$\nIf $f$ is differentiable at $c$, $g$ is differentiable at $f(c)$, and the\ncomposition is defined near $c$, then $g\\circ f$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n$(g\\circ f)'(c)=g'(f(c))f'(c)$. For every integer $m\\ge1$,\n$(x^m)'=mx^{m-1}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-second-derivative-characterises-convexity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f:I\\to\\mathbb R$ is twice differentiable on an open interval, then $f$ is convex if and only if $f''(x)\\ge0$ for every $x\\in I$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-mean-value-theorem-on-closed-interval",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on\n$(a,b)$, with $a<b$. Then there is $c\\in(a,b)$ such that\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=f'(c)(b-a).\n$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The positive-part function is nondecreasing and $1$-Lipschitz: if two inputs have the same sign, the positive-part difference is either their difference or zero; if they have opposite signs, the positive part of the nonnegative input is at most the distance between them. It is convex as the maximum of the affine functions $0$ and $1+t$. Therefore $\\ell_{\\rm h}$ is nondecreasing, convex, and $1$-Lipschitz, and $\\ell_{\\rm h}(0)=1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "By [F2]–[F3] and the chain rule, $$\\ell_{\\rm log}'(t)=\\frac{e^t}{(1+e^t)\\ln2},\\qquad \\ell_{\\rm log}''(t)=\\frac{e^t}{(1+e^t)^2\\ln2}.$$",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "By [F1], $\\ln2>0$, and [F2] gives $e^t>0$. Step 1.2 therefore shows that $0<\\ell_{\\rm log}'(t)\\le1/\\ln2$ for every real $t$. The derivative is positive, so the logistic loss is strictly increasing; applying [F5] on each closed interval gives the global $1/\\ln2$ Lipschitz bound.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "step 1.2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "The second derivative in step 1.2 is nonnegative because its numerator and denominator are positive. The convexity criterion [F4] gives convexity of $\\ell_{\\rm log}$. Also $\\ell_{\\rm log}(0)=\\log_2(2)=1$ from the definition of $\\log_2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Since $L_0$ exceeds both Lipschitz constants, steps 1.1 and 2.1 give the common $L_0$-Lipschitz bound. For the hinge loss, if $0<R<1/\\sqrt2$, then $B_{\\ell_{\\rm h},R}=(1+2R^2)-(1-2R^2)=4R^2>0$; if $R\\ge1/\\sqrt2$, then $B_{\\ell_{\\rm h},R}=1+2R^2>0$. The logistic loss is strictly increasing by step 2.1, so $B_{\\ell_{\\rm log},R}>0$ as well. In either case, the interval has length $4R^2$, so the Lipschitz bound gives $B_{\\ell,R}\\le4L_0R^2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
