    "lem-vector-rademacher-contraction-for-bounded-classes": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-cauchy-schwarz-for-finite-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For real random variables $X,Y$ on a finite probability space,\n$$\\mathbb E[XY]^2\\le\\mathbb E[X^2]\\mathbb E[Y^2].$$\nNo equality characterization is asserted on outcomes of probability zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For any $u\\in\\mathbb R^d$, put $X=\\sum_{t=1}^d\\eta_tu_t$ for independent uniform signs $\\eta_t$. Expanding the finite sign average gives $\\mathbb E X^2=\\sum_tu_t^2=\\lVert u\\rVert_2^2$ and $\\mathbb E X^4=\\sum_tu_t^4+6\\sum_{s<t}u_s^2u_t^2=3\\lVert u\\rVert_2^4-2\\sum_tu_t^4\\le3\\lVert u\\rVert_2^4$. By [F1], $\\mathbb E|X|^2\\le(\\mathbb E|X|)^{1/2}(\\mathbb E|X|^3)^{1/2}$ and $\\mathbb E|X|^3\\le(\\mathbb E X^2)^{1/2}(\\mathbb E X^4)^{1/2}$. If $u\\ne0$, combining and squaring these inequalities yields $\\mathbb E|X|\\ge(\\mathbb E X^2)^{3/2}/(\\mathbb E X^4)^{1/2}\\ge\\lVert u\\rVert_2/\\sqrt3$; the same bound is immediate for $u=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Fix an index $i$ and any real function $a$ on $V$. Averaging over $\\varepsilon_i=\\pm1$ gives half the sum of $\\sup_v(h_i(v_i)+a(v))$ and $\\sup_w(-h_i(w_i)+a(w))$, hence half the supremum over ordered pairs $(v,w)$ of $h_i(v_i)-h_i(w_i)+a(v)+a(w)$. Swapping $v,w$ leaves $a(v)+a(w)$ unchanged and reverses the loss difference, so this ordered-pair supremum equals $\\mathbb E_{\\varepsilon_i}\\sup_{v\\in V}\\{\\varepsilon_i h_i(v_i)+a(v)\\}=\\frac12\\sup_{v,w\\in V}\\{|h_i(v_i)-h_i(w_i)|+a(v)+a(w)\\}$. By Lipschitz continuity and step 1.1, the right side is at most $\\frac12\\mathbb E_{\\eta_i}\\sup_{v,w\\in V}\\{\\sqrt3L|\\langle\\eta_i,v_i-w_i\\rangle|+a(v)+a(w)\\}$. For each fixed sign vector, swapping $v,w$ shows the supremum is unchanged if the absolute value is removed. It then separates into a sum of two suprema; symmetry of $\\eta_i$ makes their expectations equal. Therefore $\\mathbb E_{\\varepsilon_i}\\sup_{v\\in V}\\{\\varepsilon_i h_i(v_i)+a(v)\\}\\le\\mathbb E_{\\eta_i}\\sup_{v\\in V}\\{\\sqrt3L\\langle\\eta_i,v_i\\rangle+a(v)\\}$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "For finite $V$, define $Q_k$ to be the expectation of the supremum with the first $k$ terms replaced by $\\sqrt3L\\sum_t\\eta_{it}v_{it}$ and the remaining terms equal to $\\varepsilon_i h_i(v_i)$. Then $Q_0$ is the left side of the Statement and $Q_m$ is its right side. For each $k<m$, condition on all signs except $\\varepsilon_{k+1}$ and the corresponding vector signs, and collect the other terms into $a(v)$. Step 2.1 gives $Q_k\\le Q_{k+1}$. Iterating these finitely many inequalities yields $Q_0\\le Q_m$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-4.1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "For arbitrary bounded nonempty $V$, the scalar sign cube has finitely many elements. Given $\\delta>0$, choose for each scalar sign vector one point of $V$ whose left-hand signed sum is within $\\delta$ of its supremum, and let $V_0$ be the finite set of chosen points. Then the left average over $V_0$ is within $\\delta$ of that over $V$, while the coordinate-sign supremum over $V_0$ is no larger than that over $V$. Applying step 3.1 to $V_0$ and letting $\\delta\\downarrow0$ proves the claimed inequality for $V$. This uses only finitely many approximate choices for each $\\delta$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "given",
            "choose",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
