    "lem-contrastive-uniform-generalization": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "thm-rademacher-uniform-generalization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice (AC). Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space, let $m\\ge1$, and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. Take $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ to be the coordinate sample with law $P^m$. Put $P_mg=m^{-1}\\sum_i g(Z_i)$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}:=\\{1-g:g\\in\\mathcal G\\}$. Assume the sample, independent-copy and sign suprema over $\\mathcal G$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}$ used in the displayed claims and the symmetrization argument are measurable. Let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs, and let\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i g(Z_i),\\qquad \\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G),$$\n\nwhere the $\\sigma_i$ are independent uniform signs, and let $\\delta\\in(0,1)$. Each of the following four guarantees holds with probability at least $1-\\delta$ over $S$:\n\n$$\\forall g\\in\\mathcal G,\\qquad Pg\\le P_mg+2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(1/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\forall g\\in\\mathcal G,\\qquad Pg\\le P_mg+2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}|Pg-P_mg|\\le2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}|Pg-P_mg|\\le2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(4/\\delta)}{2m}}.$$\n\nThe four guarantees are separate probability statements. The choice axiom is used through the bounded-differences theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-latent-class-contrastive-learning",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal C,\\mathcal C_0,\\rho)$ be a measurable probability space and $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ a measurable space. For each class $c\\in\\mathcal C$, let $D_c$ be a probability measure on $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ and assume $c\\mapsto D_c(A)$ is measurable for every $A\\in\\mathcal A$. Assume the displayed mixtures define probability measures. The positive-pair law $D_{\\rm sim}$ and negative marginal $D_{\\rm neg}$ are specified by\n\n$$D_{\\rm sim}(A\\times B):=\\int_{\\mathcal C}D_c(A)D_c(B)\\,\\rho(dc),\\qquad D_{\\rm neg}(A):=\\int_{\\mathcal C}D_c(A)\\,\\rho(dc)$$\n\nfor measurable $A,B\\in\\mathcal A$. Equivalently, draw $C\\sim\\rho$ and then draw $X,X^+$ independently with law $D_C$ for a positive pair; draw a fresh independent class $C'\\sim\\rho$ and $X^-\\sim D_{C'}$ for one negative. The class labels are latent: the learner observes the pair and negative data, not their classes.\n\nLet $d\\ge1$, $R>0$, and let $\\mathcal F$ be a nonempty class of measurable maps $f:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^d$ satisfying $\\sup_{x\\in\\mathcal X}\\lVert f(x)\\rVert_2\\le R$. Use either one-negative loss $\\ell_{\\rm h}(t)=(1+t)_+$ or $\\ell_{\\rm log}(t)=\\log_2(1+e^t)$ from the immediately preceding loss-properties lemma. The contrastive score and population loss are\n\n$$q_f(x,x^+,x^-):=f(x)^\\top\\bigl(f(x^-)-f(x^+)\\bigr),\\qquad L_{\\rm un}(f):=\\mathbb E_{(X,X^+)\\sim D_{\\rm sim},\\,X^-\\sim D_{\\rm neg}}\\bigl[\\ell(q_f(X,X^+,X^-))\\bigr].$$\n\nFor $M\\ge1$, an observed sample is $S=((x_j,x_j^+,x_j^-))_{j=1}^M$ of $M$ independent triplets with common law $D_{\\rm sim}\\otimes D_{\\rm neg}$, and its empirical loss is $\\widehat L_{\\rm un}(f):=M^{-1}\\sum_{j=1}^M\\ell(q_f(x_j,x_j^+,x_j^-))$. For this sample define the unnormalized vector Rademacher complexity\n\n$$\\mathcal R_S(\\mathcal F):=\\mathbb E_\\varepsilon\\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal F}\\sum_{j=1}^M\\sum_{t=1}^d\\bigl(\\varepsilon_{jt0}f_t(x_j)+\\varepsilon_{jt+}f_t(x_j^+)+\\varepsilon_{jt-}f_t(x_j^-)\\bigr),$$\n\nwhere every $\\varepsilon_{jt0},\\varepsilon_{jt+},\\varepsilon_{jt-}$ is an independent uniform sign. This is the one-sided convention with no absolute value and no $1/M$ factor.\n\nAll vector and matrix expectations in this definition are taken entrywise in finite dimensions. The bound on $f$ makes each coordinate of $f(X)$ and every product defining a covariance entry integrable.\n\nLet $C_+,C_-\\sim\\rho$ be independent class draws and set $\\tau:=\\Pr(C_+=C_-)<1$. Assume the diagonal $\\Delta:=\\{(c,c):c\\in\\mathcal C\\}$ and each $\\Delta_A:=\\{(c,c):c\\in A\\}$, $A\\in\\mathcal C_0$, are measurable. The average binary task draws $(C_+,C_-)$ conditional on $C_+\\ne C_-$, then samples a label $Y$ uniformly from $\\{+1,-1\\}$ and an input $X$ from $D_{C_+}$ if $Y=+1$ or $D_{C_-}$ if $Y=-1$. For a vector representation $f$, a binary linear classifier uses a weight vector $w\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and loss $\\ell(-Yw^\\top f(X))$. Define $L_{\\rm sup}(f)$ as the average over tasks of the infimum of this risk over $w\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Write $\\mu_c:=\\mathbb E_{X\\sim D_c}f(X)$ and define the class-mean classifier by $w_\\mu=\\mu_{C_+}-\\mu_{C_-}$; its average risk is $L^\\mu_{\\rm sup}(f)$. For $\\gamma>0$, write $L^\\mu_{\\gamma,\\rm sup}(f)$ for the same mean-classifier risk with margin loss $\\ell_\\gamma(t):=(1+t/\\gamma)_+$ in place of $\\ell$.\n\nFor $\\tau>0$, let $L^=_{\\rm un}(f)$ and $L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f)$ be the conditional contrastive risks given $C_+=C_-$ and $C_+\\ne C_-$, respectively. Let $\\nu$ be the class law conditioned on the collision event, namely $\\nu(A):=(\\rho\\otimes\\rho)(\\Delta_A)/\\tau$ for $A\\in\\mathcal C_0$; for an atomic class law this gives $\\nu(\\{c\\})=\\rho(\\{c\\})^2/\\tau$. Define $\\Sigma_f(c):=\\mathbb E_{X\\sim D_c}[(f(X)-\\mu_c)(f(X)-\\mu_c)^\\top]$ and\n\n$$s(f):=\\mathbb E_{C\\sim\\nu}\\left[\\sqrt{\\lVert\\Sigma_f(C)\\rVert_{\\rm op}}\\,\\mathbb E_{X\\sim D_C}\\lVert f(X)\\rVert_2\\right],\\qquad \\lVert\\Sigma\\rVert_{\\rm op}:=\\max_{\\lVert u\\rVert_2=1}\\lVert\\Sigma u\\rVert_2.$$\n\nThe assumed measurability of $\\Delta$ and every $\\Delta_A$ makes the displayed collision event and conditional class law well-defined; disjoint class sets give disjoint $\\Delta_A$, so the formula for $\\nu$ is countably additive and has total mass one. Assume also that $c\\mapsto\\mu_c$ and $c\\mapsto\\Sigma_f(c)$ are measurable for each $f$, and that the displayed class-conditional risks and random suprema used below are measurable.\n\nIf $\\tau=0$, there is no collision term; set $s(f):=0$ and use $L_{\\rm un}(f)=L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f)$. Any empirical minimizer or approximate minimizer used below is separately assumed to be a measurable selector; no selector-existence claim is part of this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in [[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]] agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in [[def-independent-random-elements]]. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-independent-random-elements-have-product-joint-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, and let\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ for $i<n$ be independent\nrandom elements. Define\n\n$$X=(X_0,\\dots,X_{n-1}):\\Omega\\to\\prod_{i<n}S_i.$$\n\nThen $X$ is a random element of\n$\\left(\\prod_{i<n}S_i,\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\Sigma_i\\right)$, and its law is the\nfinite product of the marginal laws:\n\n$$\\mathbb P_X=\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathbb P_{X_i}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-latent-class-contrastive-learning",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal C,\\mathcal C_0,\\rho)$ be a measurable probability space and $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ a measurable space. For each class $c\\in\\mathcal C$, let $D_c$ be a probability measure on $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ and assume $c\\mapsto D_c(A)$ is measurable for every $A\\in\\mathcal A$. Assume the displayed mixtures define probability measures. The positive-pair law $D_{\\rm sim}$ and negative marginal $D_{\\rm neg}$ are specified by\n\n$$D_{\\rm sim}(A\\times B):=\\int_{\\mathcal C}D_c(A)D_c(B)\\,\\rho(dc),\\qquad D_{\\rm neg}(A):=\\int_{\\mathcal C}D_c(A)\\,\\rho(dc)$$\n\nfor measurable $A,B\\in\\mathcal A$. Equivalently, draw $C\\sim\\rho$ and then draw $X,X^+$ independently with law $D_C$ for a positive pair; draw a fresh independent class $C'\\sim\\rho$ and $X^-\\sim D_{C'}$ for one negative. The class labels are latent: the learner observes the pair and negative data, not their classes.\n\nLet $d\\ge1$, $R>0$, and let $\\mathcal F$ be a nonempty class of measurable maps $f:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^d$ satisfying $\\sup_{x\\in\\mathcal X}\\lVert f(x)\\rVert_2\\le R$. Use either one-negative loss $\\ell_{\\rm h}(t)=(1+t)_+$ or $\\ell_{\\rm log}(t)=\\log_2(1+e^t)$ from the immediately preceding loss-properties lemma. The contrastive score and population loss are\n\n$$q_f(x,x^+,x^-):=f(x)^\\top\\bigl(f(x^-)-f(x^+)\\bigr),\\qquad L_{\\rm un}(f):=\\mathbb E_{(X,X^+)\\sim D_{\\rm sim},\\,X^-\\sim D_{\\rm neg}}\\bigl[\\ell(q_f(X,X^+,X^-))\\bigr].$$\n\nFor $M\\ge1$, an observed sample is $S=((x_j,x_j^+,x_j^-))_{j=1}^M$ of $M$ independent triplets with common law $D_{\\rm sim}\\otimes D_{\\rm neg}$, and its empirical loss is $\\widehat L_{\\rm un}(f):=M^{-1}\\sum_{j=1}^M\\ell(q_f(x_j,x_j^+,x_j^-))$. For this sample define the unnormalized vector Rademacher complexity\n\n$$\\mathcal R_S(\\mathcal F):=\\mathbb E_\\varepsilon\\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal F}\\sum_{j=1}^M\\sum_{t=1}^d\\bigl(\\varepsilon_{jt0}f_t(x_j)+\\varepsilon_{jt+}f_t(x_j^+)+\\varepsilon_{jt-}f_t(x_j^-)\\bigr),$$\n\nwhere every $\\varepsilon_{jt0},\\varepsilon_{jt+},\\varepsilon_{jt-}$ is an independent uniform sign. This is the one-sided convention with no absolute value and no $1/M$ factor.\n\nAll vector and matrix expectations in this definition are taken entrywise in finite dimensions. The bound on $f$ makes each coordinate of $f(X)$ and every product defining a covariance entry integrable.\n\nLet $C_+,C_-\\sim\\rho$ be independent class draws and set $\\tau:=\\Pr(C_+=C_-)<1$. Assume the diagonal $\\Delta:=\\{(c,c):c\\in\\mathcal C\\}$ and each $\\Delta_A:=\\{(c,c):c\\in A\\}$, $A\\in\\mathcal C_0$, are measurable. The average binary task draws $(C_+,C_-)$ conditional on $C_+\\ne C_-$, then samples a label $Y$ uniformly from $\\{+1,-1\\}$ and an input $X$ from $D_{C_+}$ if $Y=+1$ or $D_{C_-}$ if $Y=-1$. For a vector representation $f$, a binary linear classifier uses a weight vector $w\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and loss $\\ell(-Yw^\\top f(X))$. Define $L_{\\rm sup}(f)$ as the average over tasks of the infimum of this risk over $w\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Write $\\mu_c:=\\mathbb E_{X\\sim D_c}f(X)$ and define the class-mean classifier by $w_\\mu=\\mu_{C_+}-\\mu_{C_-}$; its average risk is $L^\\mu_{\\rm sup}(f)$. For $\\gamma>0$, write $L^\\mu_{\\gamma,\\rm sup}(f)$ for the same mean-classifier risk with margin loss $\\ell_\\gamma(t):=(1+t/\\gamma)_+$ in place of $\\ell$.\n\nFor $\\tau>0$, let $L^=_{\\rm un}(f)$ and $L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f)$ be the conditional contrastive risks given $C_+=C_-$ and $C_+\\ne C_-$, respectively. Let $\\nu$ be the class law conditioned on the collision event, namely $\\nu(A):=(\\rho\\otimes\\rho)(\\Delta_A)/\\tau$ for $A\\in\\mathcal C_0$; for an atomic class law this gives $\\nu(\\{c\\})=\\rho(\\{c\\})^2/\\tau$. Define $\\Sigma_f(c):=\\mathbb E_{X\\sim D_c}[(f(X)-\\mu_c)(f(X)-\\mu_c)^\\top]$ and\n\n$$s(f):=\\mathbb E_{C\\sim\\nu}\\left[\\sqrt{\\lVert\\Sigma_f(C)\\rVert_{\\rm op}}\\,\\mathbb E_{X\\sim D_C}\\lVert f(X)\\rVert_2\\right],\\qquad \\lVert\\Sigma\\rVert_{\\rm op}:=\\max_{\\lVert u\\rVert_2=1}\\lVert\\Sigma u\\rVert_2.$$\n\nThe assumed measurability of $\\Delta$ and every $\\Delta_A$ makes the displayed collision event and conditional class law well-defined; disjoint class sets give disjoint $\\Delta_A$, so the formula for $\\nu$ is countably additive and has total mass one. Assume also that $c\\mapsto\\mu_c$ and $c\\mapsto\\Sigma_f(c)$ are measurable for each $f$, and that the displayed class-conditional risks and random suprema used below are measurable.\n\nIf $\\tau=0$, there is no collision term; set $s(f):=0$ and use $L_{\\rm un}(f)=L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f)$. Any empirical minimizer or approximate minimizer used below is separately assumed to be a measurable selector; no selector-existence claim is part of this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-contrastive-hinge-and-logistic-loss-properties",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Write $\\ln$ for the natural logarithm and $\\log_2 u:=\\ln(u)/\\ln(2)$ for $u>0$. Define the one-negative hinge and logistic losses by $\\ell_{\\rm h}(t):=(1+t)_+$ and $\\ell_{\\rm log}(t):=\\log_2(1+e^t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$, where $r_+:=\\max\\{r,0\\}$. Put $L_0:=1+1/\\ln(2)$. Each loss is convex, nondecreasing, $L_0$-Lipschitz, and has value $1$ at $0$. For $R>0$ and either loss $\\ell$, define $B_{\\ell,R}:=\\ell(2R^2)-\\ell(-2R^2)$. Then $0<B_{\\ell,R}\\le4L_0R^2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-rademacher-uniform-generalization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice (AC). Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space, let $m\\ge1$, and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. Take $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ to be the coordinate sample with law $P^m$. Put $P_mg=m^{-1}\\sum_i g(Z_i)$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}:=\\{1-g:g\\in\\mathcal G\\}$. Assume the sample, independent-copy and sign suprema over $\\mathcal G$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}$ used in the displayed claims and the symmetrization argument are measurable. Let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs, and let\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i g(Z_i),\\qquad \\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G),$$\n\nwhere the $\\sigma_i$ are independent uniform signs, and let $\\delta\\in(0,1)$. Each of the following four guarantees holds with probability at least $1-\\delta$ over $S$:\n\n$$\\forall g\\in\\mathcal G,\\qquad Pg\\le P_mg+2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(1/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\forall g\\in\\mathcal G,\\qquad Pg\\le P_mg+2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}|Pg-P_mg|\\le2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}|Pg-P_mg|\\le2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(4/\\delta)}{2m}}.$$\n\nThe four guarantees are separate probability statements. The choice axiom is used through the bounded-differences theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-vector-rademacher-contraction-for-bounded-classes",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m,d\\ge1$, let $V$ be a nonempty bounded subset of $(\\mathbb R^d)^m$, and let $h_i:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ be $L$-Lipschitz for one common $L\\ge0$. Let $(\\varepsilon_i)_{i=1}^m$ and $(\\eta_{it})_{1\\le i\\le m,1\\le t\\le d}$ be independent uniform signs in $\\{-1,+1\\}$. Writing $v_i=(v_{i1},\\ldots,v_{id})$, the one-sided averages satisfy\n\n$$\\mathbb E_\\varepsilon\\sup_{v\\in V}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\varepsilon_i h_i(v_i)\\le\\sqrt3L\\,\\mathbb E_\\eta\\sup_{v\\in V}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{t=1}^d\\eta_{it}v_{it}.$$\n\nNeither supremum uses an absolute value, and no condition $h_i(0)=0$ is imposed. The bound holds for finite or infinite bounded $V$; all displayed suprema are finite.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For vectors $u,v$ in a real or complex inner product space,\n\n$$|\\langle u,v\\rangle|\\le\\lVert u\\rVert\\lVert v\\rVert.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if $u$ and $v$ are linearly dependent, including the case in which either vector is zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The class $L^1(\\mu)$ is a complex vector space, and the Lebesgue integral is\ncomplex-linear on it:\n$$\\int (\\alpha f+\\beta g)\\,d\\mu=\\alpha\\int f\\,d\\mu+\\beta\\int g\\,d\\mu \\qquad(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C,\\ f,g\\in L^1(\\mu)).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Set $a=\\ell(-2R^2)$ and $B=B_{\\ell,R}$. For each triplet $z=(x,x^+,x^-)$, define $g_f(z)=(\\ell(q_f(z))-a)/B$. By [F2], $g_f$ takes values in $[0,1]$. Since the shift $a$ is common to every $f$, averaging over the signs shows that the empirical one-sided Rademacher complexity of $\\{g_f:f\\in\\mathcal F\\}$ equals $B^{-1}$ times that of $\\{\\ell(q_f):f\\in\\mathcal F\\}$: the extra term is $-(a/B)\\sum_j\\varepsilon_j$, whose sign expectation is zero.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "For a fixed triplet, write $v=(u_0,u_+,u_-)\\in(\\mathbb R^d)^3$ and $h(v)=\\ell(u_0^\\top(u_--u_+))$ on $K=(\\overline B_R)^3$, where $\\overline B_R=\\{u:\\|u\\|_2\\le R\\}$. For $v,v'\\in K$, expansion of the score difference and [F5] give $|h(v)-h(v')|\\le L_0R(2\\|u_0-u'_0\\|_2+\\|u_--u'_-\\|_2+\\|u_+-u'_+\\|_2)\\le\\sqrt6L_0R\\|v-v'\\|_2$. The last inequality is Cauchy–Schwarz for the three nonnegative coordinate distances. This Lipschitz function extends to all of $(\\mathbb R^d)^3$ with the same constant by $\\widetilde h(v)=\\inf_{w\\in K}\\{h(w)+\\sqrt6L_0R\\|v-w\\|_2\\}$: on $K$ the Lipschitz inequality gives equality with $h$, and the triangle inequality gives the global Lipschitz bound.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For the observed triplets, let $V=\\{(f(x_j),f(x_j^+),f(x_j^-))_{j=1}^M:f\\in\\mathcal F\\}$, a nonempty bounded trace set. Apply [F4] to the extensions in step 1.2 with $L=\\sqrt6L_0R$. The independent coordinate signs are exactly those defining $\\mathcal R_S(\\mathcal F)$, so $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\{g_f:f\\in\\mathcal F\\})\\le\\frac{\\sqrt3\\sqrt6L_0R\\,\\mathcal R_S(\\mathcal F)}{BM}=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}L_0R\\,\\mathcal R_S(\\mathcal F)}{BM}$. The normalization $1/M$ comes from the empirical complexity convention; $\\mathcal R_S$ itself is unnormalized.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Apply [F3] to the normalized class in step 1.1. With probability at least $1-\\delta$, its uniform deviation is at most twice the complexity in step 2.1 plus $3\\sqrt{\\ln(4/\\delta)/(2M)}$. Multiplying by $B$ converts that deviation to the unnormalized losses and yields exactly $\\Gamma_M(S,\\delta)$ for every $f\\in\\mathcal F$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
