    "thm-contrastive-representation-transfer-with-class-collisions": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "lem-contrastive-empirical-minimizer-excess",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and the hypotheses of the one-negative uniform-generalization lemma for a sample $S$ of size $M$. Let $\\widehat f$ be a measurable selector taking values in $\\mathcal F$ almost surely and satisfying, almost surely, $\\widehat L_{\\rm un}(\\widehat f)\\le\\widehat L_{\\rm un}(f)+\\eta$ for every $f\\in\\mathcal F$, where $\\eta\\ge0$. For every $0<\\delta<1$, with probability at least $1-\\delta$,\n\n$$\\forall f\\in\\mathcal F,\\qquad L_{\\rm un}(\\widehat f)\\le L_{\\rm un}(f)+2\\Gamma_M(S,\\delta)+\\eta,$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\\Gamma_M(S,\\delta):=\\frac{2\\sqrt{18}L_0R\\,\\mathcal R_S(\\mathcal F)}{M}+3B_{\\ell,R}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\ln(4/\\delta)}{2M}}$$\n\nis the uniform-deviation quantity for the same sample and loss. The case $\\eta=0$ includes any attained measurable empirical minimizer; the statement does not assert that such a selector exists.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-contrastive-empirical-minimizer-excess",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and the hypotheses of the one-negative uniform-generalization lemma for a sample $S$ of size $M$. Let $\\widehat f$ be a measurable selector taking values in $\\mathcal F$ almost surely and satisfying, almost surely, $\\widehat L_{\\rm un}(\\widehat f)\\le\\widehat L_{\\rm un}(f)+\\eta$ for every $f\\in\\mathcal F$, where $\\eta\\ge0$. For every $0<\\delta<1$, with probability at least $1-\\delta$,\n\n$$\\forall f\\in\\mathcal F,\\qquad L_{\\rm un}(\\widehat f)\\le L_{\\rm un}(f)+2\\Gamma_M(S,\\delta)+\\eta,$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\\Gamma_M(S,\\delta):=\\frac{2\\sqrt{18}L_0R\\,\\mathcal R_S(\\mathcal F)}{M}+3B_{\\ell,R}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\ln(4/\\delta)}{2M}}$$\n\nis the uniform-deviation quantity for the same sample and loss. The case $\\eta=0$ includes any attained measurable empirical minimizer; the statement does not assert that such a selector exists.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-mean-classifier-loss-from-contrastive-loss",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the one-negative latent-class model has $\\tau<1$. Let $C_+,C_-$ be independent with law $\\rho$, conditioned on $C_+\\ne C_-$, let $X,X^+$ be independent with law $D_{C_+}$, and let $X^-$ be independent with law $D_{C_-}$. For every measurable bounded representation $f$ and either source hinge or logistic loss, the average binary-task mean-classifier risk satisfies\n\n$$L^\\mu_{\\rm sup}(f)\\le L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f).$$\n\nHere each binary task uses a uniform label from its two classes, and $L^\\ne_{\\rm un}$ is the contrastive loss conditional on different positive and negative classes.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-contrastive-class-collision-deviation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the one-negative latent-class model, for every bounded representation $f$ and either source loss, the population contrastive loss decomposes as\n\n$$L_{\\rm un}(f)=\\tau L^=_{\\rm un}(f)+(1-\\tau)L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f).$$\n\nIf $\\tau>0$, the same-class conditional loss obeys\n\n$$1\\le L^=_{\\rm un}(f)\\le1+c_0s(f),\\qquad c_0:=\\sqrt2\\,L_0,$$\n\nwhere $L_0$ is the common Lipschitz constant of the hinge and logistic losses. If $\\tau=0$, then $L_{\\rm un}(f)=L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f)$ and the model's convention is $s(f)=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "On the event in [F1], the empirical-minimizer excess bound holds simultaneously for all comparators $f$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "By [F3], if $\\tau>0$ then $L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(\\widehat f)=\\frac{L_{\\rm un}(\\widehat f)-\\tau L^=_{\\rm un}(\\widehat f)}{1-\\tau}\\le\\frac{L_{\\rm un}(\\widehat f)-\\tau}{1-\\tau}$. If $\\tau=0$, the same inequality is equality because $L_{\\rm un}=L^\\ne_{\\rm un}$. Since $1-\\tau>0$, these divisions are valid.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Apply [F2] to $\\widehat f$ and then use step 1.2 and [F1]. For every comparator on the event in step 1.1, $L^\\mu_{\\rm sup}(\\widehat f)\\le L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(\\widehat f)\\le\\frac{L_{\\rm un}(\\widehat f)-\\tau}{1-\\tau}\\le\\frac{L_{\\rm un}(f)-\\tau+2\\Gamma_M(S,\\delta)}{1-\\tau}$. The event has probability at least $1-\\delta$ by [F1], proving the claim.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
