    "thm-contrastive-representation-transfer-under-within-class-deviation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "thm-contrastive-representation-transfer-with-class-collisions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and the one-negative latent-class model with $\\tau<1$. Fix either source hinge or logistic loss and a sample size $M\\ge1$. Suppose all risks and suprema in the uniform-generalization statement are measurable and that a measurable exact empirical minimizer $\\widehat f\\in\\mathcal F$ exists, meaning $\\widehat L_{\\rm un}(\\widehat f)\\le\\widehat L_{\\rm un}(g)$ for every $g\\in\\mathcal F$ almost surely. For every $0<\\delta<1$, with probability at least $1-\\delta$ over the IID triplet sample,\n\n$$\\forall f\\in\\mathcal F,\\qquad L^\\mu_{\\rm sup}(\\widehat f)\\le\\frac{L_{\\rm un}(f)-\\tau+2\\Gamma_M(S,\\delta)}{1-\\tau},$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\\Gamma_M(S,\\delta):=\\frac{2\\sqrt{18}L_0R\\,\\mathcal R_S(\\mathcal F)}{M}+3B_{\\ell,R}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\ln(4/\\delta)}{2M}}$$\n\nis the uniform-deviation quantity for the same sample and loss. This is a guarantee for the average binary task with a uniform label and the class-mean linear classifier.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-latent-class-contrastive-learning",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal C,\\mathcal C_0,\\rho)$ be a measurable probability space and $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ a measurable space. For each class $c\\in\\mathcal C$, let $D_c$ be a probability measure on $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ and assume $c\\mapsto D_c(A)$ is measurable for every $A\\in\\mathcal A$. Assume the displayed mixtures define probability measures. The positive-pair law $D_{\\rm sim}$ and negative marginal $D_{\\rm neg}$ are specified by\n\n$$D_{\\rm sim}(A\\times B):=\\int_{\\mathcal C}D_c(A)D_c(B)\\,\\rho(dc),\\qquad D_{\\rm neg}(A):=\\int_{\\mathcal C}D_c(A)\\,\\rho(dc)$$\n\nfor measurable $A,B\\in\\mathcal A$. Equivalently, draw $C\\sim\\rho$ and then draw $X,X^+$ independently with law $D_C$ for a positive pair; draw a fresh independent class $C'\\sim\\rho$ and $X^-\\sim D_{C'}$ for one negative. The class labels are latent: the learner observes the pair and negative data, not their classes.\n\nLet $d\\ge1$, $R>0$, and let $\\mathcal F$ be a nonempty class of measurable maps $f:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^d$ satisfying $\\sup_{x\\in\\mathcal X}\\lVert f(x)\\rVert_2\\le R$. Use either one-negative loss $\\ell_{\\rm h}(t)=(1+t)_+$ or $\\ell_{\\rm log}(t)=\\log_2(1+e^t)$ from the immediately preceding loss-properties lemma. The contrastive score and population loss are\n\n$$q_f(x,x^+,x^-):=f(x)^\\top\\bigl(f(x^-)-f(x^+)\\bigr),\\qquad L_{\\rm un}(f):=\\mathbb E_{(X,X^+)\\sim D_{\\rm sim},\\,X^-\\sim D_{\\rm neg}}\\bigl[\\ell(q_f(X,X^+,X^-))\\bigr].$$\n\nFor $M\\ge1$, an observed sample is $S=((x_j,x_j^+,x_j^-))_{j=1}^M$ of $M$ independent triplets with common law $D_{\\rm sim}\\otimes D_{\\rm neg}$, and its empirical loss is $\\widehat L_{\\rm un}(f):=M^{-1}\\sum_{j=1}^M\\ell(q_f(x_j,x_j^+,x_j^-))$. For this sample define the unnormalized vector Rademacher complexity\n\n$$\\mathcal R_S(\\mathcal F):=\\mathbb E_\\varepsilon\\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal F}\\sum_{j=1}^M\\sum_{t=1}^d\\bigl(\\varepsilon_{jt0}f_t(x_j)+\\varepsilon_{jt+}f_t(x_j^+)+\\varepsilon_{jt-}f_t(x_j^-)\\bigr),$$\n\nwhere every $\\varepsilon_{jt0},\\varepsilon_{jt+},\\varepsilon_{jt-}$ is an independent uniform sign. This is the one-sided convention with no absolute value and no $1/M$ factor.\n\nAll vector and matrix expectations in this definition are taken entrywise in finite dimensions. The bound on $f$ makes each coordinate of $f(X)$ and every product defining a covariance entry integrable.\n\nLet $C_+,C_-\\sim\\rho$ be independent class draws and set $\\tau:=\\Pr(C_+=C_-)<1$. Assume the diagonal $\\Delta:=\\{(c,c):c\\in\\mathcal C\\}$ and each $\\Delta_A:=\\{(c,c):c\\in A\\}$, $A\\in\\mathcal C_0$, are measurable. The average binary task draws $(C_+,C_-)$ conditional on $C_+\\ne C_-$, then samples a label $Y$ uniformly from $\\{+1,-1\\}$ and an input $X$ from $D_{C_+}$ if $Y=+1$ or $D_{C_-}$ if $Y=-1$. For a vector representation $f$, a binary linear classifier uses a weight vector $w\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and loss $\\ell(-Yw^\\top f(X))$. Define $L_{\\rm sup}(f)$ as the average over tasks of the infimum of this risk over $w\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Write $\\mu_c:=\\mathbb E_{X\\sim D_c}f(X)$ and define the class-mean classifier by $w_\\mu=\\mu_{C_+}-\\mu_{C_-}$; its average risk is $L^\\mu_{\\rm sup}(f)$. For $\\gamma>0$, write $L^\\mu_{\\gamma,\\rm sup}(f)$ for the same mean-classifier risk with margin loss $\\ell_\\gamma(t):=(1+t/\\gamma)_+$ in place of $\\ell$.\n\nFor $\\tau>0$, let $L^=_{\\rm un}(f)$ and $L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f)$ be the conditional contrastive risks given $C_+=C_-$ and $C_+\\ne C_-$, respectively. Let $\\nu$ be the class law conditioned on the collision event, namely $\\nu(A):=(\\rho\\otimes\\rho)(\\Delta_A)/\\tau$ for $A\\in\\mathcal C_0$; for an atomic class law this gives $\\nu(\\{c\\})=\\rho(\\{c\\})^2/\\tau$. Define $\\Sigma_f(c):=\\mathbb E_{X\\sim D_c}[(f(X)-\\mu_c)(f(X)-\\mu_c)^\\top]$ and\n\n$$s(f):=\\mathbb E_{C\\sim\\nu}\\left[\\sqrt{\\lVert\\Sigma_f(C)\\rVert_{\\rm op}}\\,\\mathbb E_{X\\sim D_C}\\lVert f(X)\\rVert_2\\right],\\qquad \\lVert\\Sigma\\rVert_{\\rm op}:=\\max_{\\lVert u\\rVert_2=1}\\lVert\\Sigma u\\rVert_2.$$\n\nThe assumed measurability of $\\Delta$ and every $\\Delta_A$ makes the displayed collision event and conditional class law well-defined; disjoint class sets give disjoint $\\Delta_A$, so the formula for $\\nu$ is countably additive and has total mass one. Assume also that $c\\mapsto\\mu_c$ and $c\\mapsto\\Sigma_f(c)$ are measurable for each $f$, and that the displayed class-conditional risks and random suprema used below are measurable.\n\nIf $\\tau=0$, there is no collision term; set $s(f):=0$ and use $L_{\\rm un}(f)=L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f)$. Any empirical minimizer or approximate minimizer used below is separately assumed to be a measurable selector; no selector-existence claim is part of this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-contrastive-representation-transfer-with-class-collisions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and the one-negative latent-class model with $\\tau<1$. Fix either source hinge or logistic loss and a sample size $M\\ge1$. Suppose all risks and suprema in the uniform-generalization statement are measurable and that a measurable exact empirical minimizer $\\widehat f\\in\\mathcal F$ exists, meaning $\\widehat L_{\\rm un}(\\widehat f)\\le\\widehat L_{\\rm un}(g)$ for every $g\\in\\mathcal F$ almost surely. For every $0<\\delta<1$, with probability at least $1-\\delta$ over the IID triplet sample,\n\n$$\\forall f\\in\\mathcal F,\\qquad L^\\mu_{\\rm sup}(\\widehat f)\\le\\frac{L_{\\rm un}(f)-\\tau+2\\Gamma_M(S,\\delta)}{1-\\tau},$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\\Gamma_M(S,\\delta):=\\frac{2\\sqrt{18}L_0R\\,\\mathcal R_S(\\mathcal F)}{M}+3B_{\\ell,R}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\ln(4/\\delta)}{2M}}$$\n\nis the uniform-deviation quantity for the same sample and loss. This is a guarantee for the average binary task with a uniform label and the class-mean linear classifier.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-contrastive-class-collision-deviation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the one-negative latent-class model, for every bounded representation $f$ and either source loss, the population contrastive loss decomposes as\n\n$$L_{\\rm un}(f)=\\tau L^=_{\\rm un}(f)+(1-\\tau)L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f).$$\n\nIf $\\tau>0$, the same-class conditional loss obeys\n\n$$1\\le L^=_{\\rm un}(f)\\le1+c_0s(f),\\qquad c_0:=\\sqrt2\\,L_0,$$\n\nwhere $L_0$ is the common Lipschitz constant of the hinge and logistic losses. If $\\tau=0$, then $L_{\\rm un}(f)=L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f)$ and the model's convention is $s(f)=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "By the definition of $L_{\\rm sup}$ as an infimum over all linear weights, its value for $\\widehat f$ is at most the risk of the particular mean weight, so $L_{\\rm sup}(\\widehat f)\\le L^\\mu_{\\rm sup}(\\widehat f)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "On the event in [F2], fix any comparator $f$. If $\\tau>0$, [F3] gives $L_{\\rm un}(f)-\\tau=\\tau(L^=_{\\rm un}(f)-1)+(1-\\tau)L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f)\\le\\tau\\sqrt2L_0s(f)+(1-\\tau)L^\\ne_{\\rm un}(f)$. If $\\tau=0$, the same inequality is equality under the stipulated convention $s(f)=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Divide the inequality in step 1.2 by $1-\\tau>0$ and substitute it into [F2]. Together with step 1.1, this gives the displayed bound for every comparator on the same event, whose probability is at least $1-\\delta$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
